# briefing.txt -- TEMPLATE, not your real briefing

This is the structure `scoring.py` expects, based on the build spec's own
description: "verified background, hard filters, target tracks and a 0 to
100 match score method." Copy this file to `briefing.txt` (already
gitignored -- it will never be committed) and replace every section with
your real, verified content from `01_GPT_Briefing_and_Daily_Search.txt`.

This entire file is sent to Claude as the system prompt for every scoring
call (see docs/adr/ADR-002), so write it the way you'd want a sharp,
literal-minded reviewer to read it: concrete facts, explicit rules, no
inside jokes or context the model doesn't have.

---

## Part A: Verified Background

<Your real work history, credentials, quantified achievements. Only
verified facts -- this is what the model checks postings against.>

## Part B: Hard Filters

<Rules that mean an automatic "reject" regardless of score, e.g.
credential requirements you don't meet, geography, compensation floor.
NOTE: config/tracks.yaml currently has a placeholder disqualifying-phrase
list (e.g. "must be a CPA") inferred from the build spec's own test
instructions -- confirm it matches what you list here, or remove it if
it's wrong.>

## Part C: Target Tracks

### Track 1: <name>
<definition, example titles, what makes a posting a strong Track 1 fit>

### Track 2: Fractional / Interim CFO or COO
<definition, what makes a posting a strong Track 2 fit>

### Track 3: <name>
<definition, example titles, what makes a posting a strong Track 3 fit>

### Track 4: AI Operations / Transformation
<definition, what makes a posting a strong Track 4 fit, and what counts
as "applied AI/automation experience" for the AI bonus>

## Part D: Scoring Method (0-100)

<Your seven-part breakdown: name each part, its point weight, and what
earns full vs. partial vs. zero points. Also define the AI/automation
bonus separately, and the tier bands (e.g. A >= 80, B 65-79, C/reject
below that -- these should match ALERT_SCORE_A / ALERT_SCORE_B_MIN /
TRACK2_ALERT_SCORE in .env).>

## Part E: Resume Versions

<The names of your resume versions and which track/situation each is for,
so the model's "resume_version" field means something you can act on.>

## Part F: Warm Outreach Angles

<Any standing angles/connections/talking points the model should draw on
when suggesting a "warm_angle" for a posting.>

## Part G: Target Companies

<Calgary construction, development and advisory firms (and any others)
whose ATS boards should be checked every run. Add these to targets.yaml
directly, in this format:

targets:
  - company: "Real Company Name"
    board_type: greenhouse   # or lever, ashby, workable
    slug: their-board-slug
>
